README

This library provides a software reset (reboot) API for Circle applications.
A reset of the Raspberry Pi can be triggered by:

* Keyboard:	Ctrl+Alt+Del on an USB keyboard (hot-plugging is supported)
* GPIO:		an input (e.g. a push button), which is active for a given time
* Network:	an UDP command "REBOOT [password]" over Ethernet (or WLAN)
* Application:	a call to CSoftReset::RequestReset()

The trigger sources only request the reset, which is safe from any context
(also from interrupt handlers). The reset is performed in CSoftReset::Update(),
which must be called continuously from TASK_LEVEL (e.g. from the main loop).
Before the reboot an optional reset handler is called, which can be used to
bring the application into a safe state.

USAGE

	#include <softreset/softreset.h>

	CSoftReset SoftReset;

	SoftReset.RegisterResetHandler (MyResetHandler, this);	// optional
	SoftReset.EnableKeyboard ();
	SoftReset.EnableGPIO (17, TRUE, 2000);		// GPIO17 low for 2 seconds
	SoftReset.EnableNetwork (&m_Net, 5050, "secret");

	while (1)
	{
		m_USBHCI.UpdatePlugAndPlay ();		// for keyboard hot-plugging

		SoftReset.Update ();			// reboots, if requested

		// ... application work ...
	}

If you want to perform the reboot yourself (e.g. to shutdown the application
in an orderly way), call SetAutoReboot (FALSE). Then Update() returns TRUE, when
a reset has been requested, and the application can return ShutdownReboot from
CKernel::Run() or call CSoftReset::Reboot().

EnableGPIO() has an option bImmediate. If set, the system reboots directly from
the timer interrupt (without calling the reset handler), so that a reset is
possible even if the application does not call Update() any more (e.g. if it
hangs in a loop).

NETWORK PROTOCOL

The library listens on the given UDP port (default 5050), after the network is
up. Each UDP datagram contains one command (case insensitive, a trailing newline
is ignored) and is answered with one UDP datagram:

	REBOOT [password]	"OK" or "ERROR Access denied"
	PING			"PONG"

The command is not encrypted. Use it in trusted networks only. The Python script
sample/53-softreset/softreset.py can be used to send the commands.

REQUIREMENTS

* Keyboard reset: USB library (lib/usb, lib/input), CUSBHCIDevice
* Network reset: network library (lib/net), CNetSubSystem and CScheduler
* GPIO reset: CTimer (a kernel timer polls the input every 10 ms)

Libraries to be linked (in this order):

	addon/softreset/libsoftreset.a
	lib/usb/libusb.a
	lib/input/libinput.a
	lib/fs/libfs.a
	lib/net/libnet.a
	lib/sched/libsched.a
	lib/libcircle.a

Only one instance of CSoftReset is allowed. Build the library with:

	cd addon/softreset
	make
